From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 00:32:08 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: HT-PHY: don't recalibrate TX off-channel or on the
 same channel

Every HT-PHY channel switch runs the TX I/Q and LO calibration, about
17 ms of the 26 ms a switch takes. During a scan that is spent on every
channel visited, although off the operating channel only probe requests
go out, at robust rates, and again on the way back to the operating
channel, whose coefficients are still in place: the channel setup does
not touch table 13 nor the radio's coarse LO.

Remember the channel the coefficients were found on. Skip the
calibration with IEEE80211_CONF_OFFCHANNEL and when tuning to that
channel again. PHY init reloads table 13 and radio init resets the
coarse LO, so forget the channel at both. Remember it only if the
calibration finished, so that one that timed out is run again on the
next channel switch.

On a BCM4331 associated on channel 36, a channel switch now takes about
10 ms. A full scan takes 14.9 s instead of 17.6 s, ping loss during it
drops from 12% to 7% and its 95th percentile from about 350 to 210 ms.
Throughput after scans is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01S2DU22Uxo4LiJPr66K7TVt
Signed-off-by: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.h |  3 +++
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c
@@ -1274,6 +1274,7 @@ static int b43_phy_ht_set_channel(struct
 				  enum nl80211_channel_type channel_type)
 {
 	struct b43_phy *phy = &dev->phy;
+	struct b43_phy_ht *phy_ht = phy->ht;
 
 	const struct b43_phy_ht_channeltab_e_radio2059 *chent_r2059 = NULL;
 
@@ -1296,7 +1297,17 @@ static int b43_phy_ht_set_channel(struct
 		return -ESRCH;
 	}
 
-	b43_phy_ht_cal_tx_iq_lo(dev);
+	/*
+	 * Off the operating channel (scanning, remain-on-channel) only short
+	 * management frames are sent, at robust rates; keep the coefficients
+	 * of the operating channel instead of spending ~17 ms per channel.
+	 * Coming back to the channel they were found on, they still apply.
+	 * A calibration that timed out is tried again on the next tune.
+	 */
+	if (!(dev->wl->hw->conf.flags & IEEE80211_CONF_OFFCHANNEL) &&
+	    phy_ht->txcal_freq != channel->center_freq)
+		phy_ht->txcal_freq = b43_phy_ht_cal_tx_iq_lo(dev) ?
+				     channel->center_freq : 0;
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -1417,6 +1428,7 @@ static int b43_phy_ht_op_init(struct b43
 	}
 
 	b43_phy_ht_tables_init(dev);
+	phy_ht->txcal_freq = 0;
 
 	b43_phy_mask(dev, 0x0be, ~0x2);
 	b43_phy_set(dev, 0x23f, 0x7ff);
@@ -1571,6 +1583,8 @@ static void b43_phy_ht_op_software_rfkil
 			b43_radio_2059_init(dev);
 		else
 			B43_WARN_ON(1);
+		/* Radio init resets the coarse LO the calibration set */
+		dev->phy.ht->txcal_freq = 0;
 
 		/*
 		 * b43_phy_init() tunes the channel once the PHY is set up;
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.h
@@ -142,6 +142,9 @@ struct b43_phy_ht {
 
 	/* 2.4 GHz CCK (802.11b) reception is off */
 	bool cck_rx_off;
+
+	/* Channel the TX I/Q and LO coefficients in use were found on, or 0 */
+	u16 txcal_freq;
 };
 
 
